to compete for talent, and this coupled with a softening economy has meant more people working in early education.\n\nBut a new threat is looming. In 2023, it’s estimated that “the end of the pandemic grants will force 43% of child care centers to raise tuition” (source: https://lnkd.in/g_XizqJm). Parents already have trouble affording childcare and now on the brink of a recession and potentially rising costs of care, affording care will only get harder for families.\n\nSo what will parents do? I predict we’ll see a continued trend of parents reducing their childcare expenses. This will mean shifting to a less expensive form of care (daycare/preschool instead of nanny) and/or utilizing fewer hours of whatever form of paid care they have.\n\nFor example, a parent might fill the 2 hours between the childcare/school day ending and work day ending with something like “sittervising” (source: https://lnkd.in/g_MJ9d7j) or screentime or friends & family care.\n\nAt Winnie, we’re going to focus a lot in 2023 on helping child care businesses fill their open spaces, even as fewer parents might be searching for care. And we’ll help the most vulnerable families access free childcare (https://lnkd.in/gq9WFquR), special needs care (https://lnkd.in/gYw4_ZXE), and more. We’re up for the challenge.\n\n(Thanks Fast Company for including my prediction for 2023 alongside other CEOs https://lnkd.in/g7nn7sHK.)', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '3mo', 'last_updated': '2023-05-13 00:22:17', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 129376209, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7008474413197135873', 'followers': None, 'date': None, 'description': None, 'reactions_count': None, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': "There’s a big trend at the intersection of child care and the workplace that no one is talking about👇\n\nOne of the big reasons we started Winnie almost 7 years ago is we knew the simple fact that great child care enables women to stay in the workforce. It’s disproportionately mothers who drop out of the workforce when families experience child care challenges.\n\nHowever it turns out there is a group of parents that are THREE TIMES more likely than other parents to not be able to work because of childcare challenges. This group is parents of young children with special needs.\xa0\n\nMany parents of children with special needs assume that they need to hire their own employee like a nanny, which can be expensive and out of reach for most families. These families may never think to use a tool like Winnie to search for child care. If they do search for child care, it can be challenging to find a provider who can support their child’s needs. You can see why these parents are much more likely to experience job disruptions.\n\nIt's getting worse because more and more parents are impacted by this. Why? Children with disabilities now make up an estimated 17% of the country’s population and that percentage has been steadily increasing. There are also other special needs that child care providers need to be able to accommodate that might not be strictly classified as a “disability”. We are fortunately better at early intervention than we’ve ever been which also means an acceleration in parents impacted by special needs in the early years.\n\nIn response, today at Winnie we announced we launched a search for special needs child care on Winnie. You can reach more about it on our blog (link: https://lnkd.in/gyC4SXhS) or check it out at winnie.com/special-needs.\xa0\n\nIt’s important to understand that one size does not fit all when it comes to child care. Parents of children with special needs understand this more than anyone. If we want to help these parents remain in the workforce, providers need to get more specialized with the education & care they offer and we need parents to be able to find this specialized care.", 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '3mo', 'last_updated': '2023-05-13 00:22:17', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 129376210, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7005947728371355648', 'followers': 1658, 'date': '4mo', 'description': '👀  Are you noticing this emerging child care market trend?', 'reactions_count': 6, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'There’s a new trend we’re seeing in the child care market, but you won’t find it covered in the mainstream media (at least not yet!)\xa0⬇️\n\nThe trend is consolidation. Higher Ground, a leading Montessori network of schools, announced their acquisition of NeighborSchools, an in-home daycare network / tech startup (link: https://lnkd.in/gv_KsV75). We’ve seen other rollups recently too. ChildcareCRM, a popular CRM used by child care providers, acquired a child care management platform and rebranded as a result (link: https://lnkd.in/gVHJ5KeD).\n\nFirst of all, I’ll start off with a big CONGRATS to all parties involved. There is so much work that goes on behind the scenes to make acquisitions happen and it’s an incredible feat when it can all come together and result in a deal.\xa0\n\nBut what do they mean for everyone else? I think these deals indicate the tip of the iceberg for startups in the childcare space. The child care acquisitions, mergers, and rollups will continue because new startups in the space can’t rely on VC funding anymore to survive independently. And the acquisitions aren’t just limited to startups. We’re seeing increasing acquisitions of brick and mortar daycare businesses too, like KinderCare acquiring another chain of daycares (link: https://lnkd.in/gNAhiZpf)\n\nAt Winnie, we see just how fragmented the childcare landscape is and how that is challenging for parents and daycare businesses to navigate. For an exceptionally fragmented market like childcare, it’s not necessarily a bad thing to start to see a lot more consolidation. No company is anywhere near becoming an Apple-style monopoly so I don’t think this consolidation is bad for consumers. It could be a good thing. We’ll see if I’m right. \n\n#childcare #childcarecenter #childcareprovider #earlychildhoodeducation', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '4mo', 'last_updated': '2023-05-13 00:22:17', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 129376211, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7004145189309042689', 'followers': 1658, 'date': '4mo', 'description': "Winnie's CEO Sara Mauskopf writes about why childcare - despite being a big market and massive opportunity - doesn't receive the same attention from investors as say, sneaker reselling. It's time to change that!", 'reactions_count': 15, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': "Why did most venture investors question the size of the childcare market when I pitched them for funding? I have a theory\xa0⤵️\n\nFirst, a little story. Let's look at the sneaker resale market, which is valued around $6B globally and around $2B in the United States (source: https://lnkd.in/g8rPQZBq)\n\nIn 2021, StockX was valued at $3.8B (source: https://lnkd.in/gjBYaMj9) and GOAT at $3.7B (source: https://lnkd.in/gGVtzBRH). So these two sneaker resale companies together were valued at more than the entire sneaker resale market, globally.\xa0🤯 \n\nHow can this be? How can 2 companies within a market be worth more than the entire market? Well, as an investor, you have to believe - beyond what the data would tell you - that the size of the market can increase thanks to these companies. You have to believe the company you're investing in will not just achieve 100% market share, but it can actually make the market much much bigger than it is already.\n\nLots of investors believed this in 2021 about StockX and GOAT. They both have a ton of brand name investors. From what I can tell, most of them are men. Men are the ones who had this gut feeling that these companies would be big. The products appealed to them. They liked the problem being solved. They were willing to place a bet these companies would dramatically expand the market without data to prove it.\n\nChild care is a much much larger market than sneaker reselling. It's a $136B market in the United States alone and even more if you look at the entire care economy or look outside the United States (source: https://lnkd.in/gtQcyg4N)\n\nIs that enough? It's never going to be enough for male investors who don't care about solving problems that disproportionately impact women and mothers. It's not about the size of the market. It never was.", 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '4mo', 'last_updated': '2023-05-13 00:22:17', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 175518174, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7061740280403611648', 'followers': 1685, 'date': '2d', 'description': "🎉 It's teacher appreciation week! \n\nWhy is it important to show appreciation for teachers? \n\nFor starters, teaching can be a challenging and often thankless job. Many teachers spend countless hours outside of their regular workday preparing lessons, grading papers, and attending meetings. They often spend their own money on classroom supplies and materials. By showing our appreciation, we let them know that their hard work does not go unnoticed.\n\nSo, what unique gifts can you give your child's teacher during Teacher Appreciation Week? Head to the link below for ideas that will make them feel loved and appreciated.\n\nhttps://lnkd.in/ePJQQqEv", 'reactions_count': 3, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2023-06-20 14:26:29', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 175518175, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7054903057691615232', 'followers': 1685, 'date': '3w', 'description': 'Some thoughts from our CEO on the gender pay gap and what employers can do about it.', 'reactions_count': 8, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': "A study came out this week that goes part of the way to explaining the gender pay gap. It turns out that even when wives outearn their husbands, they take on more of the domestic labor at home. \n\nFrom this article https://lnkd.in/gNCGbpmV “When husbands and wives earn about the same, husbands are spending three to four hours more a week working and in leisure activities, while wives are spending two hours more on caregiving and nearly three more hours doing housework.”\n\nWhile the article doesn't go into detail on potential solutions, there's an opportunity for companies to step up and play a role in addressing this issue. It’s impossible for men to take on more household responsibilities if their job doesn’t allow for it or deem it acceptable. By implementing policies that promote a healthy work-life balance, businesses can foster a more equitable distribution of caregiving and household responsibilities, for everyone. \n\nSome strategies companies can consider include:\n🌟 Offering flexible working hours to accommodate family needs\n🌟  Supporting remote work options, enabling employees to better manage their time between work and home\n🌟  Providing paid parental leave for both parents, encouraging a more equal share of caregiving duties from the start\n\nEven simple things like asking both men and women about their children and families! It shouldn’t just be women who are constantly asked about their kids and home life in a professional setting.\n\nOf course there is so much individuals can do to take a look at their own household and try to divide work up more equitably, but it’s not on families alone. Employers should also be part of the picture. How is your employer part of the solution here?", 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '3w', 'last_updated': '2023-06-20 14:26:29', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 175518176, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7052620534005518336', 'followers': 1685, 'date': '3w', 'description': 'Have you ever considered a career in childcare and early education?\n\nWorking in a daycare can be a rewarding and fulfilling career choice. Not only does it provide a sense of purpose and the opportunity to impact young lives positively, but it also offers a range of benefits that can improve your personal and professional life.\n\nOne of the most obvious benefits of working in a daycare is the chance to work with children. If you love being around kids and enjoy watching them grow and develop, a career in childcare is a great choice. \n\nYou will get to play an important role in shaping the lives of young children and helping them learn and grow. You will also have the opportunity to help children develop important life skills, such as problem-solving, communication, and socialization.\n\nWant to learn more about the benefits of working in early education? Head here: \n\nhttps://lnkd.in/ejturCQG\n\n#work #opportunity #education #career #help', 'reactions_count': 6, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2023-06-20 14:26:29', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 175518177, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7043934332364746752', 'followers': None, 'date': None, 'description': None, 'reactions_count': None, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': '☀️ We just launched summer camp search on Winnie ☀️ \n\nIf you’re still looking for child care over the summer, check it out! And if you’re a childcare business, you can now showcase your summer programs to families on Winnie. https://lnkd.in/gTbxYh3r', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '1mo', 'last_updated': '2023-06-20 14:26:29', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 175518178, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7037180742925250560', 'followers': 1685, 'date': '2mo', 'description': 'The childcare requirement in the CHIPS Act is historic and this is why you should care! https://lnkd.in/gCa6PFPZ', 'reactions_count': 8, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2023-06-20 14:26:29', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 175518179, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7026342452072484865', 'followers': 1685, 'date': '3mo', 'description': "Winnie's CEO Sara Mauskopf on the best way to get press for your business (it's not what you think)", 'reactions_count': 4, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'Pitching your company to the press is the hardest and most flawed way to get press but many leaders are too scared to do what works. Let me explain.\n\nAnyone who has ever tried to pitch a story to the press knows how difficult it can be to stand out from the thousands of pitches a reporter receives. As a startup - especially one that hasn’t just completed a big fundraise (and who has these days?) - it’s nearly impossible to have a story newsworthy enough that a major publication will write about it. No one cares about your product launch, no matter how important it is to your company. No one cares about a new hire you made, even if that person is known in the tech industry. No one cares that you have some interesting proprietary data about something.\n\nThe better strategy for a startup to get press is to focus on adding to the conversation that is already out there. Look at what reporters and the world already care about, and then add your take or data or angle on top of it to make the conversation even richer.\n\nFor example, in tech, all anyone can talk about right now is layoffs, challenges raising money, startups going out of business, and valuations coming down. Can you add to that conversation? Did your company do a layoff and you have advice on how to handle it better than major tech companies that did layoffs? Maybe you’re a SaaS company that sells to tech companies and have data you can share about how companies are buying fewer seats of your SaaS product. When you can help the press augment the stories they are already working on, then suddenly you’re getting quoted, your data is getting cited, and your company is getting out there.\n\nSo why don’t more leaders do this? Frankly, I think they are worried they won’t be able to control the narrative and the truth is, they won’t. I remember when Emily Chang wanted to interview me about a controversial topic for Bloomberg TV and said other CEOs she approached all turned down the opportunity. But I got on TV and answered tough questions and got to spread the word about Winnie in the process (link: https://lnkd.in/giWERvDG). It wasn’t perfect and I’m sure a few of the things I said could have been worded better in retrospect (it was live TV after all) but I will gladly make that tradeoff for all the awareness it brought Winnie and our mission.\n\xa0\nSo there you have it. You can join into the existing conversation and zeitgeist even if your message isn’t always perfectly crafted, or you can have your perfect pitches ignored and not get to talk about the great work your company is doing. For me, the choice is easy.', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '3mo', 'last_updated': '2023-06-20 14:26:29', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 175518180, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7019035104291287040', 'followers': 1685, 'date': '4mo', 'description': None, 'reactions_count': 1, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Alex Sarlin', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/alex-sarlin', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'Education Technology | Product and Learning Design Leader', 'reshared_post_description': "In Part 2 of Edtech Insiders' Year in Review, six thought leaders and Edtech executives give their reviews of 2022 and predictions for 2023.\n\nKarl Rectanus, CEO of\xa0LearnPlatform\xa0on Evidence in Edtech \nSara Mauskopf, CEO of\xa0Winnie\xa0on Childcare and Teaching\n💛 Maria Barrera, CEO of\xa0💛 Clayful\xa0on SEL and COVID funding \nSunil Gunderia, CIO of\xa0Age of Learning\xa0on Early Childhood Edtech\nAnthony Kim, CLO of\xa0Scholarus Learning\xa0on AI and Learner Engagement\nNorma A. Padrón, PhD, CEO of\xa0EmpiricaLab Learning Tech\xa0on Workforce Development\n\nhttps://lnkd.in/gJ4wzGqX\n\nAlyssa Miller Ben Kornell #2023trends #2022review #edtech", 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '4mo', 'last_updated': '2023-06-20 14:26:29', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 175518181, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7016628709474402304', 'followers': 1685, 'date': '4mo', 'description': None, 'reactions_count': 7, 'comments_count': 1, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'Alongside other CEOs, I shared how I’m thinking about leading Winnie through this economic climate https://lnkd.in/g8ZGqktj', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '4mo', 'last_updated': '2023-06-20 14:26:29', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 175518182, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7014689880400154624', 'followers': None, 'date': None, 'description': None, 'reactions_count': None, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'Just a friendly PSA about the Dependent Care FSA before we all sign off for the year! \n\nAs we prepare to return to child care next week, we might see costs increase. If you’re already locked into a contract with a provider, this is unlikely, but those enrolling in new programs or looking to the summer or fall may see higher prices (source: https://lnkd.in/g27Y8wgu). If you aren’t hit by higher child care costs right now, you will be later.\xa0\n\nSo, as I predicted too, we’ll start to see all sorts of creative solutions to this problem from parents. This article has a few examples https://lnkd.in/gDCQsr3N and we’ve put together some of our own suggestions Winnie on how to make childcare more affordable: https://lnkd.in/gJjGegDx\n\nMany of these will depend on your own family and financial situation, but one nearly universal tip is to make sure if you have a Dependent Care FSA and that you’ve submitted your claims before the deadline! What’s a Dependent Care FSA? It’s a pre-tax benefit account used to pay for eligible dependent care services, such as daycare, preschool, summer day camp, before or after school programs, nannies, etc. It’s a really broad definition of care by design!\n\nIf you don’t have one, why not? Please comment and let me know. It is a nice savings for parents and doesn’t cost an employer much of anything to offer.\n\nOk that’s my final tip to end 2022. I’ll be back with more in 2023. Happy new year!', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '4mo', 'last_updated': '2023-06-20 14:26:29', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 175518183, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7012187225094324224', 'followers': 1685, 'date': '4mo', 'description': 'Winnie CEO Sara Mauskopf shares her 2023 prediction for the child care industry', 'reactions_count': 17, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'My prediction for the biggest challenge the childcare industry will face in 2023: reduction in child care demand. Let me explain. ⤵ \n\nFor the childcare industry, the biggest challenge it faced in 2022 was the child care staffing crisis. Child care businesses across the country struggled to hire and retain staff, in many cases closing entire classrooms. At Winnie, we responded by quickly launching https://lnkd.in/grNgimCx and saw fast adoption with child care providers because they desperately needed the hiring help.\xa0\n\nFortunately, the situation has improved. Savvy childcare businesses upped their game to compete for talent, and this coupled with a softening economy has meant more people working in early education.\n\nBut a new threat is looming. In 2023, it’s estimated that “the end of the pandemic grants will force 43% of child care centers to raise tuition” (source: https://lnkd.in/g_XizqJm). Parents already have trouble affording childcare and now on the brink of a recession and potentially rising costs of care, affording care will only get harder for families.\n\nSo what will parents do? I predict we’ll see a continued trend of parents reducing their childcare expenses. This will mean shifting to a less expensive form of care (daycare/preschool instead of nanny) and/or utilizing fewer hours of whatever form of paid care they have.\n\nFor example, a parent might fill the 2 hours between the childcare/school day ending and work day ending with something like “sittervising” (source: https://lnkd.in/g_MJ9d7j) or screentime or friends & family care.\n\nAt Winnie, we’re going to focus a lot in 2023 on helping child care businesses fill their open spaces, even as fewer parents might be searching for care. And we’ll help the most vulnerable families access free childcare (https://lnkd.in/gq9WFquR), special needs care (https://lnkd.in/gYw4_ZXE), and more. We’re up for the challenge.\n\n(Thanks Fast Company for including my prediction for 2023 alongside other CEOs https://lnkd.in/g7nn7sHK.)', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '4mo', 'last_updated': '2023-06-20 14:26:29', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 217628681, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7074454893750616064', 'followers': 1732, 'date': '6d', 'description': 'Everything you need to know about the new Pregnant Workers Fairness Act, a huge win for working mothers across the country! https://lnkd.in/gCeQ57Bw', 'reactions_count': 4, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2023-07-10 14:23:14', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 217628682, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7070491004193173504', 'followers': 1732, 'date': '2w', 'description': 'Trust and safety is so important when building a caregiving marketplace. Let’s invest in our existing caregiving infrastructure rather than trying to create new supply through quick hacks.', 'reactions_count': 5, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'Bloomberg recently published an article on eldercare marketplace Papa uncovering harassment and sexual assault perpetrated by both caregivers and care recipients on the Papa platform: https://lnkd.in/gi8hPHFe The crimes described in the article are horrific, but I’m not shocked to hear they happened. I’d argue they were very predictable and preventable based on what I know about the startup valued at $1.4 billion and the class of startups it represents.\n\nIn the past seven years of building Winnie, I’ve witnessed firsthand the emergence of marketplaces that promise to "turn anyone into a caregiver" with minimal to no training and vetting. It’s a direct reflection of a society desperate to create a greater supply of care. But in our rush to fill this growing supply gap, we’ve neglected the caregivers already doing this work today.\n\nAt Winnie we’ve taken an entirely different approach to building a care marketplace. We only work with vetted, licensed childcare businesses. It\'s unquestionably safer and more reliable than individual caregivers. Licensed childcare businesses have stringent regulations in place that require background checks, mandatory training, and inspections for anyone intending to provide childcare services. \n\nWe also believe in investing in the existing caregiving supply rather than trying to create new supply through quick hacks. At Winnie, we’ve launched a childcare jobs marketplace (https://winnie.com/jobs) to help child care workers find the best opportunities and stay in the field. Our entire mission is to strengthen existing childcare businesses and help them grow – that’s how we create supply.\n\nWe also need to recognize the unique dynamics of caregiving. Caregiving, be it eldercare or childcare, is not a service that can be scaled like an e-commerce business. The job requires specialized skills and a genuine empathy that cannot be ensured through a quick online registration process. It’s about nurturing long-term, meaningful relationships between caregivers and care recipients. Often this is at odds with the venture style growth. Papa raised $240M in venture capital at a $1.4 billion valuation. They were clearly under massive pressure to grow quickly into that inflated valuation at all costs. \n\nThe demand for caregiving will continue to rise, and it\'s true that we need innovative solutions to meet this escalating need. However, innovation should never come at the expense of safety and quality. Let\'s ensure that innovation is directed towards reinforcing our established caregiving systems.', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '2w', 'last_updated': '2023-07-10 14:23:14', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 217628683, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7065338067875729408', 'followers': 1732, 'date': '1mo', 'description': 'Winnie CEO Sara Mauskopf weighs in on the essential role of remote work in keeping women in the workforce and redefining productivity.', 'reactions_count': 14, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'Elon Musk was recently quoted as saying that working from home is “morally wrong” and unfair when service workers need to come into the office[1]. He’s not alone in trying to get people back into the office. According to recent reports[2], remote work is becoming increasingly rare. At the same time, a record number of women are leaving the workforce[3], citing a lack of flexibility and rising childcare costs. \n\nMusk and other big company leaders might believe that onsite work makes employees more productive, and they might be right, but that’s the wrong goal. The more important goal is what makes our society as a whole more productive and that’s to keep women in the workforce. A country where half of the population has to drop out or step back from the workforce is not a productive society. \n\nTo that end, remote work is a key piece of the puzzle in keeping women in the workforce. Remote work, and the flexibility it goes hand in hand with, helps make the elusive juggle between work and childcare responsibilities more manageable. We know from extensive data that when that juggle becomes unmanageable, it’s disproportionately women who are impacted.\n\nOne of the biggest advantages of giving parents this increased flexibility is that they can adjust their work hours to accommodate their childcare responsibilities. It’s often these small gaps and breakdowns in childcare that add up and push women out of the workforce. When you have remote work and flexibility in the picture, you’re able to handle smaller childcare failures without missing an entire day of work.\n\nRemote work is also a massive childcare cost savings for families. With more flexibility in terms of the start and end time of childcare, parents have a wider range of providers to choose from and therefore can find the most affordable option. With a reduced commute, a proven source of stress, parents need fewer hours of childcare as well, saving them money.\n\nFinally, there’s evidence that when men work from home, they share in more of the childcare and household responsibilities. The “invisible” labor becomes more visible and results in a more equitable division of labor.\n\nAs we hear from business leaders about how onsite work increases the productivity of their team, we must also ask who profits from this? Is it Elon Musk and or is it women and their ability to become equal earners in society? It’s clear to me why certains CEOs would push for this arrangement, but the rest of us should not fall for this ruse.\n\n1. https://lnkd.in/gtSyKExJ\n2. https://lnkd.in/gWkia3u6\n3. https://lnkd.in/gQPVGmjz', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '1mo', 'last_updated': '2023-07-10 14:23:14', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 217628684, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7061740280403611648', 'followers': 1732, 'date': '1mo', 'description': "🎉 It's teacher appreciation week! \n\nWhy is it important to show appreciation for teachers? \n\nFor starters, teaching can be a challenging and often thankless job. Many teachers spend countless hours outside of their regular workday preparing lessons, grading papers, and attending meetings. They often spend their own money on classroom supplies and materials. By showing our appreciation, we let them know that their hard work does not go unnoticed.\n\nSo, what unique gifts can you give your child's teacher during Teacher Appreciation Week? Head to the link below for ideas that will make them feel loved and appreciated.\n\nhttps://lnkd.in/ePJQQqEv", 'reactions_count': 5, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2023-07-10 14:23:14', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 217628685, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7054903057691615232', 'followers': 1732, 'date': '2mo', 'description': 'Some thoughts from our CEO on the gender pay gap and what employers can do about it.', 'reactions_count': 8, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': "A study came out this week that goes part of the way to explaining the gender pay gap. It turns out that even when wives outearn their husbands, they take on more of the domestic labor at home. \n\nFrom this article https://lnkd.in/gNCGbpmV “When husbands and wives earn about the same, husbands are spending three to four hours more a week working and in leisure activities, while wives are spending two hours more on caregiving and nearly three more hours doing housework.”\n\nWhile the article doesn't go into detail on potential solutions, there's an opportunity for companies to step up and play a role in addressing this issue. It’s impossible for men to take on more household responsibilities if their job doesn’t allow for it or deem it acceptable. By implementing policies that promote a healthy work-life balance, businesses can foster a more equitable distribution of caregiving and household responsibilities, for everyone. \n\nSome strategies companies can consider include:\n🌟 Offering flexible working hours to accommodate family needs\n🌟  Supporting remote work options, enabling employees to better manage their time between work and home\n🌟  Providing paid parental leave for both parents, encouraging a more equal share of caregiving duties from the start\n\nEven simple things like asking both men and women about their children and families! It shouldn’t just be women who are constantly asked about their kids and home life in a professional setting.\n\nOf course there is so much individuals can do to take a look at their own household and try to divide work up more equitably, but it’s not on families alone. Employers should also be part of the picture. How is your employer part of the solution here?", 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '2mo', 'last_updated': '2023-07-10 14:23:14', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 217628686, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7052620534005518336', 'followers': 1732, 'date': '2mo', 'description': 'Have you ever considered a career in childcare and early education?\n\nWorking in a daycare can be a rewarding and fulfilling career choice. Not only does it provide a sense of purpose and the opportunity to impact young lives positively, but it also offers a range of benefits that can improve your personal and professional life.\n\nOne of the most obvious benefits of working in a daycare is the chance to work with children. If you love being around kids and enjoy watching them grow and develop, a career in childcare is a great choice. \n\nYou will get to play an important role in shaping the lives of young children and helping them learn and grow. You will also have the opportunity to help children develop important life skills, such as problem-solving, communication, and socialization.\n\nWant to learn more about the benefits of working in early education? Head here: \n\nhttps://lnkd.in/ejturCQG\n\n#work #opportunity #education #career #help', 'reactions_count': 6, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2023-07-10 14:23:14', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 217628687, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7043934332364746752', 'followers': None, 'date': None, 'description': None, 'reactions_count': None, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': '☀️ We just launched summer camp search on Winnie ☀️ \n\nIf you’re still looking for child care over the summer, check it out! And if you’re a childcare business, you can now showcase your summer programs to families on Winnie. https://lnkd.in/gTbxYh3r', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '3mo', 'last_updated': '2023-08-02 12:13:04', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 217628688, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7037180742925250560', 'followers': 1732, 'date': '3mo', 'description': 'The childcare requirement in the CHIPS Act is historic and this is why you should care! https://lnkd.in/gCa6PFPZ', 'reactions_count': 8, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2023-07-10 14:23:14', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 217628689, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7026342452072484865', 'followers': 1732, 'date': '4mo', 'description': "Winnie's CEO Sara Mauskopf on the best way to get press for your business (it's not what you think)", 'reactions_count': 4, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'Pitching your company to the press is the hardest and most flawed way to get press but many leaders are too scared to do what works. Let me explain.\n\nAnyone who has ever tried to pitch a story to the press knows how difficult it can be to stand out from the thousands of pitches a reporter receives. As a startup - especially one that hasn’t just completed a big fundraise (and who has these days?) - it’s nearly impossible to have a story newsworthy enough that a major publication will write about it. No one cares about your product launch, no matter how important it is to your company. No one cares about a new hire you made, even if that person is known in the tech industry. No one cares that you have some interesting proprietary data about something.\n\nThe better strategy for a startup to get press is to focus on adding to the conversation that is already out there. Look at what reporters and the world already care about, and then add your take or data or angle on top of it to make the conversation even richer.\n\nFor example, in tech, all anyone can talk about right now is layoffs, challenges raising money, startups going out of business, and valuations coming down. Can you add to that conversation? Did your company do a layoff and you have advice on how to handle it better than major tech companies that did layoffs? Maybe you’re a SaaS company that sells to tech companies and have data you can share about how companies are buying fewer seats of your SaaS product. When you can help the press augment the stories they are already working on, then suddenly you’re getting quoted, your data is getting cited, and your company is getting out there.\n\nSo why don’t more leaders do this? Frankly, I think they are worried they won’t be able to control the narrative and the truth is, they won’t. I remember when Emily Chang wanted to interview me about a controversial topic for Bloomberg TV and said other CEOs she approached all turned down the opportunity. But I got on TV and answered tough questions and got to spread the word about Winnie in the process (link: https://lnkd.in/giWERvDG). It wasn’t perfect and I’m sure a few of the things I said could have been worded better in retrospect (it was live TV after all) but I will gladly make that tradeoff for all the awareness it brought Winnie and our mission.\n\xa0\nSo there you have it. You can join into the existing conversation and zeitgeist even if your message isn’t always perfectly crafted, or you can have your perfect pitches ignored and not get to talk about the great work your company is doing. For me, the choice is easy.', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '4mo', 'last_updated': '2023-07-10 14:23:14', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 217628690, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7019035104291287040', 'followers': 1732, 'date': '5mo', 'description': None, 'reactions_count': 1, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Alex Sarlin', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/alex-sarlin', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'Education Technology | Product and Learning Design Leader', 'reshared_post_description': "In Part 2 of Edtech Insiders' Year in Review, six thought leaders and Edtech executives give their reviews of 2022 and predictions for 2023.\n\nKarl Rectanus, CEO of\xa0LearnPlatform\xa0on Evidence in Edtech \nSara Mauskopf, CEO of\xa0Winnie\xa0on Childcare and Teaching\n💛 Maria Barrera, CEO of\xa0💛 Clayful\xa0on SEL and 