aid Sara Mauskopf, CEO and co-founder at one of the largest online child care marketplaces\xa0Winnie. \n\n👉 Read more here https://lnkd.in/g5-DYkPB', 'reactions_count': 4, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-07-11 04:15:22', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 610272736, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7190871841643663360', 'followers': None, 'date': '1mo', 'description': 'Price transparency increases enrollment rates by 33% for childcare centers!', 'reactions_count': 22, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': "Imagine not knowing the rent for an apartment until you showed up and toured it. One thing child care providers get wrong is that it's better to withhold any pricing information until a parent tours their center. We have data from Winnie to show that keeping prices a secret is hurting childcare businesses by increasing operational costs and lowering enrollment rates. \n\nIn the competitive world of childcare, transparency isn't just a good practice—it's a strategic advantage. Sharing pricing upfront not only improves search rankings and increases parental interest, but it also leads to a 33% increase in enrollment rates for centers that do so. \n\nIt's an outdated practice to keep prices hidden and it's disadvantaging the entire childcare industry as well as families. Hopefully by sharing the business impact we can advocate for more price transparency in childcare https://lnkd.in/gCgAaHj6", 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '1mo', 'last_updated': '2024-07-11 04:15:22', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 610272737, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7189842402948386817', 'followers': None, 'date': '1mo', 'description': 'Miss our group chat with Sara Mauskopf from Winnie earlier this week?\n\nThis was a really great group chat with with Sara where we she shared some of her key learnings from Postmates, the founding story for Winnie, what the first steps were that they took to start it as an app for parents to discover activities for kids nearby, what led to their pivot into childcare, we did a deep dive into it as a leading childcare marketplace today, got to learn more about their recent growth, and also had a great group Q&A.\n\nThe recording is now live!\n\nhttps://lnkd.in/gH_sB_7g', 'reactions_count': 7, 'comments_count': 1, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-07-11 04:15:22', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 610272738, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7189300090006614017', 'followers': None, 'date': '1mo', 'description': "I've been a speaker at the ASU+GSV Summit for many years, but this year's panel stood out as my favorite because WE WENT THERE and had a lively debate around some controversial topics in early education like funding models, the impact of AI, and the role the government should play.\n\nI had the honor of speaking alongside brilliant leaders like Chris Bennett (Wonderschool), John Olsen (Teaching Strategies, LLC), Ben Newton (Vivvi), and Matt Greenfield (Rethink Education). The panel was about innovative solutions for making high-quality early childhood education both accessible and affordable.\n\nWe delved into the controversial topic of whether a private versus public funding model best serves the future of early education. We talked about how society undervalues early education and (IMHO) overvalues higher ed. And some of us disagreed about how AI can/should transform childcare. Check out the full discussion here:", 'reactions_count': 49, 'comments_count': 3, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-07-11 04:15:22', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 610272739, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7166943276225290240', 'followers': None, 'date': '3mo', 'description': "If you're an employer interested in providing a childcare benefit that's far more affordable and flexible than an onsite center, we'd love to talk! https://lnkd.in/gFTEqEA4", 'reactions_count': 7, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': "Not sure if people saw this amid all the layoff news, but Google and other companies have closed their onsite childcare centers.\n\nGoogle cited cost-cutting as the reason and General Mills cited a post-pandemic drop in usage as families preferred closer options. \n\nCompanies that sell child care benefits to employers should think outside the box in terms of what solutions they offer. A one size fits all approach like an onsite center isn't going to work when workers are distributed, or when cost is a concern. And if cost is a concern for a trillion dollar company like Google, trust me it is for every company.\n\nAt Winnie, we're betting that employers who want to provide childcare benefits will look to solutions that are more flexible and take advantage of the existing supply of child care providers, rather than trying to stand up entirely new centers. Time will tell if we're right!", 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '3mo', 'last_updated': '2024-07-11 04:15:22', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 610272740, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7150668728257445888', 'followers': None, 'date': '5mo', 'description': 'Winnie’s CEO Sara Mauskopf spoke at CES on the importance of childcare to help moms and all parents return to the workforce after having a baby 🤗', 'reactions_count': 4, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'The Female Quotient', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/company/femalequotient', 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': 'The healthcare industry and workplaces across the U.S. have largely overlooked new mothers in their postpartum phase, but technology is helping to change that for good. New personalized apps, wearable devices like hands-free breast pumps, and community platforms for new moms combined with flexible work arrangements are redefining what the transition back to work looks like for mothers.\n\nWatch live from the #EqualityLounge at #CES2024 for a conversation about how technology is creating a bridge between home and office life for working mothers:\n\n• Kerry Bailey, US General Manager, Elvie\n• Sara Mauskopf, Co-Founder and CEO, Winnie\n• Abby Mercado, Co-Founder, CEO, Rescripted', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '5mo', 'last_updated': '2024-07-11 04:15:22', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 610272741, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7150573222088822784', 'followers': None, 'date': '5mo', 'description': '2% of VC funding goes to female founders. 12% of AI researchers are women. 33% of the employees at large tech companies are women. Let’s shape the future of tech and create the Algorithm for Equality®. Join me in @The Female Quotient\'s #EqualityLounge @ #CES2024 – the place for conscious leaders changing the equation and closing the gender gap. RSVP: https://lnkd.in/gXCTeuRd In case you can\'t be there in person, tune in on The Female Quotient\'s LinkedIn!\n\nI\'m moderating a panel "How Technology Is Redefining Return to Work for Moms" with the amazing Sara Mauskopf, the Co-Founder and CEO of Winnie, and Kerry Bailey, the US General Manager of Elvie, today 1/9 at 3 pm PT in the ARIA Sky Suites ✨', 'reactions_count': 47, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-07-11 04:15:22', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 610272742, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7128103204449386497', 'followers': None, 'date': '7mo', 'description': "Many people don't know that daycare can be a franchise businesses. The franchisor owns the brand,\xa0and a franchisee pays a royalty and often an initial fee for the right to do business under the franchisor's name and system. Winnie works with many of the largest daycare franchise businesses in the United States!", 'reactions_count': 6, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'Daycare franchises represent an often overlooked opportunity for entrepreneurs looking to make a meaningful impact while also tapping into a growing market. By opening a franchise, you can take out a lot of risk of starting a child care business but still reap the rewards.\n\nUnlike other industries, the demand for quality childcare is steadily increasing as more households have two working parents and recognize the importance of early childhood education. For business owners, this translates to a venture with a stable customer base and a service that is essential and resilient, even in economic downturns.\n\nMoreover, daycare franchises offer a predictable schedule which can lead to a better work-life balance for the owner. Franchise support, including training and a proven business model, helps entrepreneurs succeed. When you open a franchise, you know roughly what to expect in terms of profit. It’s less risky than starting an entirely new daycare business because it comes with a playbook.\n\nWhile other franchise opportunities might also be profitable, you might not feel the same job satisfaction in handing people their fast food as you get from watching children learn and grow. With the expansion of daycare franchises, not only do entrepreneurs benefit, but the country does as well—increasing access to early education and enabling more parents to enter or remain in the workforce.\n\nSo, have I convinced you yet? 😉 If you were opening a franchise business, what would you be considering?', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '7mo', 'last_updated': '2024-07-11 04:15:22', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 610272743, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7124857949465808896', 'followers': None, 'date': '7mo', 'description': "More money for childcare is always a good thing. At Winnie, we're happy to talk to any employer looking to understand ways they can help their employees with childcare. Reach out!", 'reactions_count': 1, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'In all the bad news about the childcare industry recently (the childcare cliff, skyrocketing prices, shortage of childcare workers) you might have missed this REALLY GOOD NEWS.\n\nLast year, a bipartisan group passed the CHIPS Act in an effort to boost US semiconductor manufacturing. Semiconductor companies can now receive money from the federal government to build new plants in the United States. However, because of labor shortages due in large part to lack of childcare, the Commerce Department announced that any manufacturing company who wants to qualify for these subsidies must help their employees with childcare.\n\nThere will be many ways for companies to qualify for “helping with childcare” which range from building an onsite facility to money for employees to helping create more childcare options locally. \n\nSo why is this such a big deal to the childcare industry?\n\nThe childcare requirement in the CHIPS Act is recognition and funding at the federal level that parents cannot work without childcare. This is not going to be the last time that childcare is included as a provision of receiving funding. Mark my words!\n\nIf you want to learn more about the childcare landscape and the best ways to help your employees with childcare, I’d love to connect!', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '7mo', 'last_updated': '2024-07-11 04:15:22', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 610272744, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7120861862266621952', 'followers': None, 'date': '7mo', 'description': None, 'reactions_count': 5, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Blessing Oyeleye Adesiyan', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/blessingadesiyan', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'Founder & CEO @ Caring Bloc', 'reshared_post_description': "We're just days away from the\xa0Care At Work Summit, and I'm reaching out to extend a warm invitation to you. If you're still considering whether to join us, I encourage you to be a part of this remarkable event. It's a full day dedicated to challenging the norms of caregiving at home and advancing care for parents, caregivers, and all employees alike.\xa0\n\xa0\nWe're excited to welcome executives from industry-leading organizations like Amazon, Kindbody, Winnie, and many more. Together, we aim to unlock innovative care solutions for today's diverse workforce. For parents and caregivers, this event is a must-attend. It's an opportunity to gain insights on advocating for yourself in the workplace, effectively managing your caregiving and work responsibilities, and so much more. We've even prepared a reimbursement template to help you secure support for your professional development.\xa0\n\xa0\nBut it's not just for individual caregivers. This summit is perfect for managers, ERG leaders, and business executives who play a crucial role in nurturing, engaging, and supporting our dynamic workforce, where 78% are caregivers. As both men and women step into caregiving during their most active years in the workplace, it's vital to offer holistic support through various life changes. So, whether you're a caregiver or a leader responsible for supporting them, this event is designed to provide invaluable insights and strategies. \n\nJoin us at the Care At Work Summit and be a part of this transformative experience. Excited to see my long-time friend and advocate for better childcare options Sara Mauskopf next week!\n\nwww.mhworklife.com/summit", 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '8mo', 'last_updated': '2024-07-11 04:15:22', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 636702079, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7212083296418062336', 'followers': None, 'date': '1w', 'description': 'What an honor to be invited to the US Chamber of Commerce today on behalf of Winnie for the first National Child Care Innovation Summit.\n\nWe started this company over 8 years ago when everyone said the TAM was too small and our idea was too niche. Our “niche company” has the attention of the US government now. LFG!', 'reactions_count': 204, 'comments_count': 16, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-08-07 16:30:55', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 636702080, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7198711005210632192', 'followers': None, 'date': '1mo', 'description': '“Child care facilities are being forced to either find alternative business models to pay the bills or shut down,” said Sara Mauskopf, CEO and co-founder at one of the largest online child care marketplaces\xa0Winnie. \n\n👉 Read more here https://lnkd.in/g5-DYkPB'