 you yet? 😉 If you were opening a franchise business, what would you be considering?', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '9mo', 'last_updated': '2024-09-07 12:11:48', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 667197525, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7124857949465808896', 'followers': None, 'date': '9mo', 'description': "More money for childcare is always a good thing. At Winnie, we're happy to talk to any employer looking to understand ways they can help their employees with childcare. Reach out!", 'reactions_count': 1, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'In all the bad news about the childcare industry recently (the childcare cliff, skyrocketing prices, shortage of childcare workers) you might have missed this REALLY GOOD NEWS.\n\nLast year, a bipartisan group passed the CHIPS Act in an effort to boost US semiconductor manufacturing. Semiconductor companies can now receive money from the federal government to build new plants in the United States. However, because of labor shortages due in large part to lack of childcare, the Commerce Department announced that any manufacturing company who wants to qualify for these subsidies must help their employees with childcare.\n\nThere will be many ways for companies to qualify for “helping with childcare” which range from building an onsite facility to money for employees to helping create more childcare options locally. \n\nSo why is this such a big deal to the childcare industry?\n\nThe childcare requirement in the CHIPS Act is recognition and funding at the federal level that parents cannot work without childcare. This is not going to be the last time that childcare is included as a provision of receiving funding. Mark my words!\n\nIf you want to learn more about the childcare landscape and the best ways to help your employees with childcare, I’d love to connect!', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '9mo', 'last_updated': '2024-09-07 12:11:48', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 703460131, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7237911245759074306', 'followers': None, 'date': '16h', 'description': "Trump was recently asked (by Reshma Saujani of Moms First!!) how he was going to make childcare more affordable for families. He gave a word salad of an answer about taxing foreign nations, and his daughter Ivanka, and other stuff that made no sense. So I've fixed it for him. Both Trump and Harris should feel free to use my answer at the upcoming debate as childcare in a bipartisan issue.\n\nFirst, expand the Child Tax Credit. Giving more money to families will help them afford the high prices especially during a child's first five years of life. (Wondering just how much it costs? Check out Winnie's cost calculator at winnie.com/cost-calculator)\n\nSecond, give more funding to child care providers. Child care providers run very low margin businesses and don't get much/any government support the way schools do. Some relief funding like what was provided during the pandemic would go a long way.\n\nFinally, just reduce the amount of regulation that exists in child care. In the state of California, for example, to be a preschool teacher you need an Associate's/Bachelor's Degree plus at least 12 semester units of coursework in early childhood education. That is really expensive and takes years to get. There's also a lot of regulation associated with opening and running a childcare business that doesn't actually make the business any safer but sure is expensive.\n\nThose are just a few ideas. What are yours?", 'reactions_count': 51, 'comments_count': 5, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-09-19 07:17:02', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 703460132, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7229424623036088322', 'followers': None, 'date': '3w', 'description': 'I’m excited to share that Winnie is No. 275 on the #Inc5000 list of the fastest-growing private companies by revenue. This is the second year in a row that Winnie has made the list. \n\nI don’t really care much for lists and some seem to be a negative signal (30 under 30 anyone?) but the #Inc5000 list is not subjective. Instead, it’s an objective calculation based on a company’s revenue growth. The number is audited so this isn’t like a fundraising pitch where companies can exaggerate (we never did that anyway though).\n\nWe continue to grow year over year because we are helping our customers (child care businesses) grow their revenue. I’m not ashamed to say we are making a lot of money by helping our customers make a lot of money. The child care industry is an attractive one and I wish more entrepreneurs and investors of all kinds understood this.\n\nChildcare is too expensive for most families but as we enable more thriving, successful childcare businesses, we 1) enable more options for families at a variety of price points, 2) attract more high quality teachers/staff to the field of early education, and 3) make it easier for businesses to accept subsidies from the government and employers. Thriving child care businesses are a good thing for families so let’s make child care businesses strong!\n\nOk back to work. You can see the full #Inc5000 list here: https://inc.com/inc5000', 'reactions_count': 205, 'comments_count': 14, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-09-19 07:17:02', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 703460133, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7227348190545465344', 'followers': None, 'date': '4w', 'description': "Still pinching myself! Yesterday, on Moms Equal Pay Day, a small group of us gathered with the First Partner of California, Jennifer Siebel Newsom, to talk openly and honestly about how to best support women in the workplace. \n\nWe met on the site of Guidepost Montessori of Emeryville, a beautiful campus caring for kids from 12 weeks to 6 years old - the perfect spot to talk about the importance of childcare and how it's critical infrastructure for mothers and families.\n\nI left the discussion feeling so invigorated. First, it was awesome to hear about how Guidepost has built so many thriving childcare centers (~150!) across the country and world. The news contains so much doom and gloom about the childcare industry but here's a model that supports families, teachers, and provides a great foundation for children. So many awesome businesses like this exist and are thriving!\n\nSecond, it was great to get so much support from the public sector. I was told by the First Partner how much California values innovation in the private sector and how much the govenor's office wants to collaborate with Winnie and see the company thrive. Wow that felt good!", 'reactions_count': 76, 'comments_count': 3, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-09-19 07:17:02', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 703460134, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7226989590018940929', 'followers': None, 'date': '1mo', 'description': "Today we launched a childcare cost calculator at Winnie to show just how much childcare costs based on where you live: https://lnkd.in/gPzaYSy5 Childcare is clearly expensive, but even more so when you take a pay cut just for becoming a mother.\n\nToday is #MomsEqualPay day, the day that raises awareness of the pay penalty mothers experience in the workplace. At Winnie, we are committed to paying our employees fairly, no matter their parenthood status. That's why we have taken the Equal Pay Pledge, reaffirming our dedication to closing the pay gap and ensuring fair compensation for all employees.\n\nRead more here about how you can take the equal pay pledge, and why childcare might FEEL more expensive than it really is when moms simultaneously take a pay cut just as they need to pay for care. https://lnkd.in/gnhRFWVY", 'reactions_count': 160, 'comments_count': 6, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-11-04 02:38:56', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 703460135, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7212083296418062336', 'followers': None, 'date': '2mo', 'description': 'What an honor to be invited to the US Chamber of Commerce today on behalf of Winnie for the first National Child Care Innovation Summit.\n\nWe started this company over 8 years ago when everyone said the TAM was too small and our idea was too niche. Our “niche company” has the attention of the US government now. LFG!', 'reactions_count': 206, 'comments_count': 16, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-10-17 06:06:07', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 703460136, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7198711005210632192', 'followers': None, 'date': '3mo', 'description': '“Child care facilities are being forced to either find alternative business models to pay the bills or shut down,” said Sara Mauskopf, CEO and co-founder at one of the largest online child care marketplaces\xa0Winnie. \n\n👉 Read more here https://lnkd.in/g5-DYkPB', 'reactions_count': 4, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None