ounding story for Winnie, what the first steps were that they took to start it as an app for parents to discover activities for kids nearby, what led to their pivot into childcare, we did a deep dive into it as a leading childcare marketplace today, got to learn more about their recent growth, and also had a great group Q&A.\n\nThe recording is now live!\n\nhttps://lnkd.in/gH_sB_7g', 'reactions_count': 7, 'comments_count': 1, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-10-17 06:06:07', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 703460139, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7189300090006614017', 'followers': None, 'date': '4mo', 'description': "I've been a speaker at the ASU+GSV Summit for many years, but this year's panel stood out as my favorite because WE WENT THERE and had a lively debate around some controversial topics in early education like funding models, the impact of AI, and the role the government should play.\n\nI had the honor of speaking alongside brilliant leaders like Chris Bennett (Wonderschool), John Olsen (Teaching Strategies, LLC), Ben Newton (Vivvi), and Matt Greenfield (Rethink Education). The panel was about innovative solutions for making high-quality early childhood education both accessible and affordable.\n\nWe delved into the controversial topic of whether a private versus public funding model best serves the future of early education. We talked about how society undervalues early education and (IMHO) overvalues higher ed. And some of us disagreed about how AI can/should transform childcare. Check out the full discussion here:", 'reactions_count': 49, 'comments_count': 3, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-09-19 07:17:02', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 703460140, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7166943276225290240', 'followers': None, 'date': '6mo', 'description': "If you're an employer interested in providing a childcare benefit that's far more affordable and flexible than an onsite center, we'd love to talk! https://lnkd.in/gFTEqEA4", 'reactions_count': 7, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': "Not sure if people saw this amid all the layoff news, but Google and other companies have closed their onsite childcare centers.\n\nGoogle cited cost-cutting as the reason and General Mills cited a post-pandemic drop in usage as families preferred closer options. \n\nCompanies that sell child care benefits to employers should think outside the box in terms of what solutions they offer. A one size fits all approach like an onsite center isn't going to work when workers are distributed, or when cost is a concern. And if cost is a concern for a trillion dollar company like Google, trust me it is for every company.\n\nAt Winnie, we're betting that employers who want to provide childcare benefits will look to solutions that are more flexible and take advantage of the existing supply of child care providers, rather than trying to stand up entirely new centers. Time will tell if we're right!", 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '6mo', 'last_updated': '2024-09-19 07:17:02', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 730495880, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7240394983441059840', 'followers': None, 'date': '5d', 'description': 'Childcare & Remote Work\n\nRemote work has reshaped childcare needs, but not always for the better, especially for those balancing career and caregiving.\n\nWinnie’s Sara Mauskopf and Alex Wilhelm break it down:\n\nRecent data shows women aged 25-44 are more likely to work remotely than men, driven by a need to juggle childcare - often at the cost of career progression.\n\nAccording to the Bureau of Labor Statistics:\n-- Women 25 to 34 are 28% remote\n-- Women 35 to 44 are 30% remote\n-- 7-8% higher than men in the same demographic', 'reactions_count': 33, 'comments_count': 7, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-10-17 06:06:07', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 730495881, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7240142811113648129', 'followers': None, 'date': '5d', 'description': "The Call for Innovation in Childcare\n\nThe childcare market has a massive TAM but still lacks competition and innovation. Winnie is stepping up to improve the industry for parents, kids, and caregivers.\n\nWith a $60B market, there's room for new players.\n\nAnd it's not just about profit; it's about improving the future for parents, kids, and caregivers.", 'reactions_count': 49, 'comments_count': 13, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-10-17 06:06:07', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 730495882, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7237911245759074306', 'followers': None, 'date': '1w', 'description': "Trump was recently asked (by Reshma Saujani of Moms First!!) how he was going to make childcare more affordable for families. He gave a word salad of an answer about taxing foreign nations, and his daughter Ivanka, and other stuff that made no sense. So I've fixed it for him. Both Trump and Harris should feel free to use my answer at the upcoming debate as childcare is a bipartisan issue.\n\nFirst, expand the Child Tax Credit. Giving more money to families will help them afford the high prices especially during a child's first five years of life. (Wondering just how much it costs? Check out Winnie's cost calculator at winnie.com/cost-calculator)\n\nSecond, give more funding to child care providers. Child care providers run very low margin businesses and don't get much/any government support the way schools do. Some relief funding like what was provided during the pandemic would go a long way.\n\nFinally, just reduce the amount of regulation that exists in child care. In the state of California, for example, to be a preschool teacher you need an Associate's/Bachelor's Degree plus at least 12 semester units of coursework in early childhood education. That is really expensive and takes years to get. There's also a lot of regulation associated with opening and running a childcare business that doesn't actually make the business any safer but sure is expensive.\n\nThose are just a few ideas. What are yours?", 'reactions_count': 87, 'comments_count': 9, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-10-17 06:06:07', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 730495883, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7229424623036088322', 'followers': None, 'date': '1mo', 'description': 'I’m excited to share that Winnie is No. 275 on the #Inc5000 list of the fastest-growing private companies by revenue. This is the second year in a row that Winnie has made the list. \n\nI don’t really care much for lists and some seem to be a negative signal (30 under 30 anyone?) but the #Inc5000 list is not subjective. Instead, it’s an objective calculation based on a company’s revenue growth. The number is audited so this isn’t like a fundraising pitch where companies can exaggerate (we never did that anyway though).\n\nWe continue to grow year over year because we are helping our customers (child care businesses) grow their revenue. I’m not ashamed to say we are making a lot of money by helping our customers make a lot of money. The child care industry is an attractive one and I wish more entrepreneurs and investors of all kinds understood this.\n\nChildcare is too expensive for most families but as we enable more thriving, successful childcare businesses, we 1) enable more options for families at a variety of price points, 2) attract more high quality teachers/staff to the field of early education, and 3) make it easier for businesses to accept subsidies from the government and employers. Thriving child care businesses are a good thing for families so let’s make child care businesses strong!\n\nOk back to work. You can see the full #Inc5000 list here: https://inc.com/inc5000', 'reactions_count': 207, 'comments_count': 14, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-10-17 06:06:07', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 730495884, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7227348190545465344', 'followers': None, 'date': '1mo', 'description': "Still pinching myself! Yesterday, on Moms Equal Pay Day, a small group of us gathered with the First Partner of California, Jennifer Siebel Newsom, to talk openly and honestly about how to best support women in the workplace. \n\nWe met on the site of Guidepost Montessori of Emeryville, a beautiful campus caring for kids from 12 weeks to 6 years old - the perfect spot to talk about the importance of childcare and how it's critical infrastructure for mothers and families.\n\nI left the discussion feeling so invigorated. First, it was awesome to hear about how Guidepost has built so many thriving childcare centers (~150!) across the country and world. The news contains so much doom and gloom about the childcare industry but here's a model that supports families, teachers, and provides a great foundation for children. So many awesome businesses like this exist and are thriving!\n\nSecond, it was great to get so much support from the public sector. I was told by the First Partner how much California values innovation in the private sector and how much the govenor's office wants to collaborate with Winnie and see the company thrive. Wow that felt good!", 'reactions_count': 76, 'comments_count': 3, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-11-04 02:38:56', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 730495885, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7198711005210632192', 'followers': None, 'date': '4mo', 'description': '“Child care facilities are being forced to either find alternative business models to pay the bills or shut down,” said Sara Mauskopf, CEO and co-founder at one of the largest online child care marketplaces\xa0Winnie. \n\n👉 Read more here https://lnkd.in/g5-DYkPB', 'reactions_count': 4, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-11-04 02:38:56', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 764382287, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7240394983441059840', 'followers': None, 'date': '2w', 'description': 'Childcare & Remote Work\n\nRemote work has reshaped childcare needs, but not always for the better, especially for those balancing career and caregiving.\n\nWinnie’s Sara Mauskopf and Alex Wilhelm break it down:\n\nRecent data shows women aged 25-44 are more likely to work remotely than men, driven by a need to juggle childcare - often at the cost of career progression.\n\nAccording to the Bureau of Labor Statistics:\n-- Women 25 to 34 are 28% remote\n-- Women 35 t