venue growth. The number is audited so this isn’t like a fundraising pitch where companies can exaggerate (we never did that anyway though).\n\nWe continue to grow year over year because we are helping our customers (child care businesses) grow their revenue. I’m not ashamed to say we are making a lot of money by helping our customers make a lot of money. The child care industry is an attractive one and I wish more entrepreneurs and investors of all kinds understood this.\n\nChildcare is too expensive for most families but as we enable more thriving, successful childcare businesses, we 1) enable more options for families at a variety of price points, 2) attract more high quality teachers/staff to the field of early education, and 3) make it easier for businesses to accept subsidies from the government and employers. Thriving child care businesses are a good thing for families so let’s make child care businesses strong!\n\nOk back to work. You can see the full #Inc5000 list here: https://inc.com/inc5000', 'reactions_count': 211, 'comments_count': 15, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2025-01-24 04:52:52', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 864770277, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7227348190545465344', 'followers': None, 'date': '4mo', 'description': "Still pinching myself! Yesterday, on Moms Equal Pay Day, a small group of us gathered with the First Partner of California, Jennifer Siebel Newsom, to talk openly and honestly about how to best support women in the workplace. \n\nWe met on the site of Guidepost Montessori of Emeryville, a beautiful campus caring for kids from 12 weeks to 6 years old - the perfect spot to talk about the importance of childcare and how it's critical infrastructure for mothers and families.\n\nI left the discussion feeling so invigorated. First, it was awesome to hear about how Guidepost has built so many thriving childcare centers (~150!) across the country and world. The news contains so much doom and gloom about the childcare industry but here's a model that supports families, teachers, and provides a great foundation for children. So many awesome businesses like this exist and are thriving!\n\nSecond, it was great to get so much support from the public sector. I was told by the First Partner how much California values innovation in the private sector and how much the govenor's office wants to collaborate with Winnie and see the company thrive. Wow that felt good!", 'reactions_count': 76, 'comments_count': 3, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2025-01-24 04:52:52', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 864770278, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7226989590018940929', 'followers': None, 'date': '4mo', 'description': "Today we launched a childcare cost calculator at Winnie to show just how much childcare costs based on where you live: https://lnkd.in/gPzaYSy5 Childcare is clearly expensive, but even more so when you take a pay cut just for becoming a mother.\n\nToday is #MomsEqualPay day, the day that raises awareness of the pay penalty mothers experience in the workplace. At Winnie, we are committed to paying our employees fairly, no matter their parenthood status. That's why we have taken the Equal Pay Pledge, reaffirming our dedication to closing the pay gap and ensuring fair compensation for all employees.\n\nRead more here about how you can take the equal pay pledge, and why childcare might FEEL more expensive than it really is when moms simultaneously take a pay cut just as they need to pay for care. https://lnkd.in/gnhRFWVY", 'reactions_count': 160, 'comments_count': 6, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'r