es.\n\nHere's a reality check Dr. Dale Herl: many parents grapple with finding childcare during these days off, with many mothers leaving the workforce due to this very struggle. We've seen an uptick in searches for flexible care solutions so high that we introduced a new category for it on Winnie's homepage just this week.\n\nSo, I’m frustrated this week. It feels like we are working tirelessly to help provide parents with childcare against a system & country that Just. Doesn’t. Care. Our commitment at Winnie remains unwavering though. We'll keep pushing to ensure all children can access high-quality care and education.", 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '3mo', 'last_updated': '2024-02-23 01:42:12', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 452397509, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7111036690898583552', 'followers': None, 'date': '4mo', 'description': None, 'reactions_count': 2, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'If you haven’t yet watched this recent talk by illustrious venture capital investor Bill Gurley on Regulatory Capture, it is an excellent overview of what happens when special interests lobby for government regulation that harms society as a whole.\n\nhttps://lnkd.in/gAb-iWBp\n\nIn his talk, Gurley shares his experience building Tropos Networks, a company designed to bring mesh Wi-Fi technology to cities. Despite enthusiasm from city mayors, robust lobbying campaigns led by major telecom giants such as Verizon and Comcast ultimately killed the deals and meant residents of those cities were not able to access this better technology.\n\nHe provided other examples of industries hit by regulatory capture like healthcare and even Covid testing, and the ways that regulation benefited a special interest and harmed society.\n\nAs it turns out, the childcare industry is also impacted by regulatory capture, and as a result, parents and childcare providers\xa0pay an enormous price.\xa0I wrote more about how the childcare industry is impacted and how that makes child care SO DAMN EXPENSIVE for consumers. Please have a read. https://lnkd.in/gESjevnz', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '4mo', 'last_updated': '2024-02-23 01:42:12', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 452397510, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7106116833061060609', 'followers': None, 'date': '4mo', 'description': None, 'reactions_count': 4, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': "Labor force participation for women with kids under 5 has skyrocketed! (source: https://lnkd.in/gPjymRtB). Our work is done!!! 🎉🎉🎉 \n\nSeriously though, when Anne K. Halsall and I started working on Winnie we were motivated by the challenge of keeping mothers of young children in the workforce. So what gets the credit for all these gains?\n\nIt’s a good question. The article points out “More research needs to be done, but it looks like a big factor is remote work, which enabled more women to stay attached to the workforce.”\n\nI agree that remote work is a factor. It's great for keeping women in the workforce, not just because it’s more flexible, but because it opens up more childcare possibilities. You don’t need to find childcare just on your commute and in fact, the time saved on a commute can either be put towards a childcare commute or towards less hours of childcare needed. Either way it can be a cost savings (and cost is a big barrier when it comes to childcare).\n\nBut also, THE ECONOMY. Women need to work right now. We’re seeing this show up in the fact that it has become easier to hire childcare staff. Whether it’s remote or not, women are working.\n\nWhat do you think? Any other explanations for the surge in women with young kids in the workforce?", 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '4mo', 'last_updated': '2024-02-23 01:42:12', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 452397511, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7097212932987506690', 'followers': None, 'date': '5mo', 'description': 'Big News! Winnie is #177 on the 2023 #Inc5000, the annual list of the fastest-growing private companies in America! Check out the list here https://lnkd.in/ggUGydu and read more from our CEO & co-founder.', 'reactions_count': 18, 'comments_count': 2, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'Winnie was named the 177th fastest-growing private company on the\xa0Inc. 5000 list this year, a list of the 5,000 fastest-growing private companies in the U.S. based on their revenue growth. Winnie ranked first in the Consumer Services category and 34th in California. This is a huge accomplishment for Winnie and a testament to the vital role Winnie plays in the child care industry.\xa0\n\xa0\nAs we celebrate this achievement, I wanted to share some of the journey it took to get us here. As a founder, I think when we demystify the work it takes to build a successful business, we help pave the path for others to do the same.\n\xa0\nWe started Winnie in 2016 with the goal of making life easier for parents. We both had young children at the time and struggled as working parents to balance work and parenting responsibilities. As it turns out, we weren’t alone. One of the\xa0biggest problems\xa0working parents face is accessing and affording high-quality childcare and early education and when the juggle becomes unmanageable, it’s\xa0predominantly women\xa0who drop out of the workforce.\xa0\n\xa0\nIt took years, but we eventually built a comprehensive directory of licensed child care providers across the United States,\xa0winnie.com, to help parents better understand and access their childcare options. But even after we built a thriving website where parents could search and contact all their local daycares and preschools, it wasn’t obvious how we would turn the thriving website into a viable business. Parents wanted a website to find childcare but they didn’t necessarily want to pay for it. After all, parents already struggled to afford the childcare itself.\n\xa0\nThen it was years after launching our comprehensive childcare directory that we finally found and honed our business model. Today, our customers are childcare businesses who we help to fill their open spaces and grow their business. Because we have so many parents using Winnie to find childcare, we’re now a huge value add to the childcare businesses trying to reach those same parents. For child care providers, we do the work of marketing their business to the exact right families who would be a fit and save them a lot of time and money in the process. Doing right by our customers (and improving their profit margin) is how we too have been able to grow in spite of some macroeconomic headwinds this year.\n\xa0\nEven as our revenue has been growing steadily year over year, the journey has not been without its challenges. While other CEOs may tell the press it’s always going great, it hasn’t always been great. We aren’t an overnight success. We are, however, a business passionate about solving problems for the child care industry and we’re built to last. Thanks for being part of our journey.', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '5mo', 'last_updated': '2024-02-23 01:42:12', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 452397512, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7090367750090477568', 'followers': None, 'date': '5mo', 'description': "Today Winnie announced it's partnership with Yext! ⤵ \n\nTo make it easier for childcare businesses to be featured on Winnie with accurate, up-to-date information about all their centers, Winnie is now integrated with\xa0Yext\xa0(NYSE: YEXT). Yext is an open and composable digital experience platform that helps businesses store, manage, and centrally publish information about their business, among many other capabilities. A number of child care businesses already use Yext to maintain data about their centers, and now that data can be seamlessly integrated and shown on their Winnie pages and easily kept accurate and up to date. It saves time and effort for child care providers and is a win for parents who rely on that data!https://lnkd.in/g78hE9zS", 'reactions_count': 30, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-02-23 01:42:12', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 480374386, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7150668728257445888', 'followers': None, 'date': '1mo', 'description': 'Winnie’s CEO Sara Mauskopf spoke at CES on the importance of childcare to help moms and all parents return to the workforce after having a baby 🤗', 'reactions_count': 4, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'The Female Quotient', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/company/femalequotient', 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': 'The healthcare industry and workplaces across the U.S. have largely overlooked new mothers in their postpartum phase, but technology is helping to change that for good. New personalized apps, wearable devices like hands-free breast pumps, and community platforms for new moms combined with flexible work arrangements are redefining what the transition back to work looks like for mothers.\n\nWatch live from the #EqualityLounge at #CES2024 for a conversation about how technology is creating a bridge between home and office life for working mothers:\n\n• Kerry Bailey, US General Manager, Elvie\n• Sara Mauskopf, Co-Founder and CEO, Winnie\n• Abby Mercado, Co-Founder, CEO, Rescripted', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '1mo', 'last_updated': '2024-03-14 19:48:24', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 480374387, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7150573222088822784', 'followers': None, 'date': '1mo', 'description': '2% of VC funding goes to female founders. 12% of AI researchers are women. 33% of the employees at large tech companies are women. Let’s shape the future of tech and create the Algorithm for Equality®. Join me in @The Female Quotient\'s #EqualityLounge @ #CES2024 – the place for conscious leaders changing the equation and closing the gender gap. RSVP: https://lnkd.in/gXCTeuRd In case you can\'t be there in person, tune in on The Female Quotient\'s LinkedIn!\n\nI\'m moderating a panel "How Technology Is Redefining Return to Work for Moms" with the amazing Sara Mauskopf, the Co-Founder and CEO of Winnie, and Kerry Bailey, the US General Manager of Elvie, today 1/9 at 3 pm PT in the ARIA Sky Suites ✨', 'reactions_count': 44, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': None, 'reshared_post_author_url': None, 'reshared_post_author_headline': None, 'reshared_post_description': None, 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': None, 'last_updated': '2024-03-14 19:48:24', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 480374388, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7128103204449386497', 'followers': None, 'date': '3mo', 'description': "Many people don't know that daycare can be a franchise businesses. The franchisor owns the brand,\xa0and a franchisee pays a royalty and often an initial fee for the right to do business under the franchisor's name and system. Winnie works with many of the largest daycare franchise businesses in the United States!", 'reactions_count': 5, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'Daycare franchises represent an often overlooked opportunity for entrepreneurs looking to make a meaningful impact while also tapping into a growing market. By opening a franchise, you can take out a lot of risk of starting a child care business but still reap the rewards.\n\nUnlike other industries, the demand for quality childcare is steadily increasing as more households have two working parents and recognize the importance of early childhood education. For business owners, this translates to a venture with a stable customer base and a service that is essential and resilient, even in economic downturns.\n\nMoreover, daycare franchises offer a predictable schedule which can lead to a better work-life balance for the owner. Franchise support, including training and a proven business model, helps entrepreneurs succeed. When you open a franchise, you know roughly what to expect in terms of profit. It’s less risky than starting an entirely new daycare business because it comes with a playbook.\n\nWhile other franchise opportunities might also be profitable, you might not feel the same job satisfaction in handing people their fast food as you get from watching children learn and grow. With the expansion of daycare franchises, not only do entrepreneurs benefit, but the country does as well—increasing access to early education and enabling more parents to enter or remain in the workforce.\n\nSo, have I convinced you yet? 😉 If you were opening a franchise business, what would you be considering?', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '3mo', 'last_updated': '2024-03-14 19:48:24', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 480374389, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7124857949465808896', 'followers': None, 'date': '3mo', 'description': "More money for childcare is always a good thing. At Winnie, we're happy to talk to any employer looking to understand ways they can help their employees with childcare. Reach out!", 'reactions_count': 1, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': 'In all the bad news about the childcare industry recently (the childcare cliff, skyrocketing prices, shortage of childcare workers) you might have missed this REALLY GOOD NEWS.\n\nLast year, a bipartisan group passed the CHIPS Act in an effort to boost US semiconductor manufacturing. Semiconductor companies can now receive money from the federal government to build new plants in the United States. However, because of labor shortages due in large part to lack of childcare, the Commerce Department announced that any manufacturing company who wants to qualify for these subsidies must help their employees with childcare.\n\nThere will be many ways for companies to qualify for “helping with childcare” which range from building an onsite facility to money for employees to helping create more childcare options locally. \n\nSo why is this such a big deal to the childcare industry?\n\nThe childcare requirement in the CHIPS Act is recognition and funding at the federal level that parents cannot work without childcare. This is not going to be the last time that childcare is included as a provision of receiving funding. Mark my words!\n\nIf you want to learn more about the childcare landscape and the best ways to help your employees with childcare, I’d love to connect!', 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '3mo', 'last_updated': '2024-03-14 19:48:24', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 480374390, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7120861862266621952', 'followers': None, 'date': '4mo', 'description': None, 'reactions_count': 4, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Blessing Oyeleye Adesiyan', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/blessingadesiyan', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'Founder of MH WorkLife +  Executive Director of Caring Africa  | Care Economy Innovator & Board Advisor| Chemical Engineer, Gender Economy & Sustainability Champion', 'reshared_post_description': "We're just days away from the\xa0Care At Work Summit, and I'm reaching out to extend a warm invitation to you. If you're still considering whether to join us, I encourage you to be a part of this remarkable event. It's a full day dedicated to challenging the norms of caregiving at home and advancing care for parents, caregivers, and all employees alike.\xa0\n\xa0\nWe're excited to welcome executives from industry-leading organizations like Amazon, Kindbody, Winnie, and many more. Together, we aim to unlock innovative care solutions for today's diverse workforce. For parents and caregivers, this event is a must-attend. It's an opportunity to gain insights on advocating for yourself in the workplace, effectively managing your caregiving and work responsibilities, and so much more. We've even prepared a reimbursement template to help you secure support for your professional development.\xa0\n\xa0\nBut it's not just for individual caregivers. This summit is perfect for managers, ERG leaders, and business executives who play a crucial role in nurturing, engaging, and supporting our dynamic workforce, where 78% are caregivers. As both men and women step into caregiving during their most active years in the workplace, it's vital to offer holistic support through various life changes. So, whether you're a caregiver or a leader responsible for supporting them, this event is designed to provide invaluable insights and strategies. \n\nJoin us at the Care At Work Summit and be a part of this transformative experience. Excited to see my long-time friend and advocate for better childcare options Sara Mauskopf next week!\n\nwww.mhworklife.com/summit", 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '4mo', 'last_updated': '2024-04-05 09:51:07', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 480374391, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7113600298358632448', 'followers': None, 'date': '4mo', 'description': None, 'reactions_count': 4, 'comments_count': None, 'reshared_post_author': 'Sara Mauskopf', 'reshared_post_author_url': 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/smausk', 'reshared_post_author_headline': 'CEO and Co-Founder, Winnie', 'reshared_post_description': "This week, a headline caught my attention: many school districts across the U.S. are transitioning to a 4-day school week. This is incredibly dangerous for this country. ⬇ \n\nConsidering the setbacks children already faced during the Covid era, cutting down on time in school only makes matters worse. Beyond the academic implications, it also intensifies childcare challenges for families.\n\nA statement from Superintendent Dale Herl in the article minimizes the pressing childcare concerns many parents face: “You have to go back and look, you know, what do parents do during the summertime? What do they do over, you know, spring break or Christmas break?” he said, adding that schools already had weekdays off for occasions such as teacher conferences.\n\nHere's a reality check Dr. Dale Herl: many parents grapple with finding childcare during these days off, with many mothers leaving the workforce due to this very struggle. We've seen an uptick in searches for flexible care solutions so high that we introduced a new category for it on Winnie's homepage just this week.\n\nSo, I’m frustrated this week. It feels like we are working tirelessly to help provide parents with childcare against a system & country that Just. Doesn’t. Care. Our commitment at Winnie remains unwavering though. We'll keep pushing to ensure all children can access high-quality care and education.", 'reshared_post_followers': None, 'reshared_post_date': '4mo', 'last_updated': '2024-03-14 19:48:24', 'deleted': 1}, {'id': 480374392, 'company_id': 9752392, 'urn': 'urn:li:activity:7111036690898583552', 'followers': None, 'date': '5mo', 